Two scripts drive a real Virtaal window to generate screenshots, rather
than relying on hand-captures that go stale silently:

    generate_appdata_screenshots.py   docs/_static/appdata/*.png
    generate_docs_screenshots.py      docs/_static/*.png (screenshots.rst)

Both take --write (regenerate into the real output directory) or --check
(regenerate into a temp dir, compare against what's committed, exit
non-zero on drift). CI runs --check on every build-flatpak run and
uploads freshly generated images as a build artifact whenever they've
drifted - both checks are non-blocking, so a stale screenshot never fails
the build. If CI flags drift, download the artifact and commit the
refreshed PNGs - see docs/_static/appdata/README for why that should
always come from CI's own Linux run, not a local capture on another OS.

testenv.py holds setup shared by both scripts: isolating $HOME so a run
can never touch a real user's Virtaal config, forcing a consistent light
theme regardless of the host's own dark-mode setting, and parking the
mouse cursor clear of the window on a real X11 session.

content/appdata/ and content/docs/ hold each script's own purpose-built
fixtures - never devsupport/testfiles/, which is shared pytest fixture
territory too easy to disturb by mistake for an unrelated test change.
Each fixture is named for what it demonstrates (checks.po, terminology.po,
...), not for its internal category (a docs- or testfiles- prefix) or the
excerpt's source language - a fixture's own filename can end up visible
on screen itself (a window title, the Welcome Screen's Recent Files
list), so it needs to read as a plausible real file, not leaked internal
plumbing. The two scripts get their own subdirectory rather than a flat
shared one so each can use its own short, natural name (e.g. a
"placeable.po") without colliding with the other's.
